/**
  @page guides-getting-started-core-concepts Core concepts
  @brief Follow the path from a geometric domain to a discrete solution.

  A Rodin solve connects five kinds of objects: a mesh, a finite element space,
  fields, a variational problem, and a solver. This guide explains their roles
  and the information passed between them. The
  @ref examples-pdes-poisson "Poisson tutorial" supplies a complete executable.

  @tableofcontents
  @m_footernavigation

  @section guides-getting-started-core-concepts-geometry Describe the domain

  @subsection guides-getting-started-core-concepts-mesh Mesh geometry and topology

  A mesh discretizes a domain into cells. Coordinates describe where the
  vertices lie; connectivity describes how vertices, edges, faces, and cells
  meet. These are related but distinct pieces of data.

  For a triangular mesh of the unit square:

  @code{.cpp}
  Mesh mesh = Mesh().UniformGrid(Polytope::Type::Triangle, {16, 16});
  mesh.scale(1.0 / 15.0);
  @endcode

  The grid parameters count vertices. There are 15 intervals per direction,
  hence 256 vertices and 450 triangles. The scaling maps unit grid spacing
  onto the unit square.

  @subsection guides-getting-started-core-concepts-polytopes Cells and ambient dimension

  A polytope describes an entity such as a segment, triangle, or tetrahedron.
  Cells have the mesh's topological dimension; boundary faces have one lower
  dimension. Ambient dimension records the number of coordinate components.
  A surface triangulation in three-dimensional space therefore has
  `getDimension() == 2` and `getSpaceDimension() == 3`.

  See @ref guides-polytopes for reference elements and geometric mappings,
  and @ref guides-meshes for mesh creation and queries.

  @section guides-getting-started-core-concepts-connectivity Establish incidence relations

  Algorithms need particular incidence relations. For boundary conditions on a
  two-dimensional mesh, edge-to-cell connectivity identifies exterior edges:

  @code{.cpp}
  mesh.getConnectivity().compute(1, 2);
  @endcode

  The arguments are entity dimensions. In a three-dimensional volume mesh,
  the corresponding face-to-cell relation is `(2, 3)`. Computing one relation
  does not mean every possible relation is available. The
  @ref guides-connectivity "connectivity guide" explains the supported queries.

  @subsection guides-getting-started-core-concepts-iterators Traverse mesh entities

  Mesh iterators expose cells, faces, and vertices. Traversal and geometric
  evaluation serve different purposes: an entity index identifies topology,
  while a `Geometry::Point` combines an entity with a reference coordinate
  for field evaluation. See @ref guides-meshes-iteration "Mesh iteration".

  @section guides-getting-started-core-concepts-variational Choose an approximation

  @subsection guides-getting-started-core-concepts-fe-spaces Finite element spaces

  A finite element space determines the local approximation and how degrees
  of freedom are shared between cells. For scalar fields on a triangular mesh:

  | Space | Approximation | Continuity |
  | :--- | :--- | :--- |
  | `P0` | Constant on each cell | Discontinuous |
  | `P1` | Affine on each cell | Continuous across cell boundaries |
  | `H1` | Configurable higher order | Conforming continuity |

  @code{.cpp}
  P1 vh(mesh);
  @endcode

  Scalar `P1` has one degree of freedom per vertex. This is a property of that
  space, not a universal rule for finite elements. Higher-order and vector
  spaces have their own numbering and degrees of freedom. The
  @ref fem-ciarlet "finite element definition" and @ref fem-spaces "space guide"
  develop this distinction. Quadratic elements use `H1` with order two;
  there is no separate `P2` class.

  @section guides-getting-started-core-concepts-gridfunctions Distinguish fields from symbolic unknowns

  A `GridFunction` stores coefficients in a finite element space. For the
  scalar nodal `P1` space, these coefficients are vertex values. Other spaces
  may use different degrees of freedom, so coefficients must not generally
  be interpreted as sampled point values.

  @subsection guides-getting-started-core-concepts-gf-create Interpolate a known field

  Assignment applies the space's degree-of-freedom functionals to an expression:

  @code{.cpp}
  GridFunction field(vh);
  field = [](const Geometry::Point& p) { return p.x() * p.y(); };
  @endcode

  This interpolates a known function; it does not solve a PDE. It is also
  distinct from an L2 projection, which requires a variational mass-matrix
  problem. See @ref guides-numerical-contracts.

  @subsection guides-getting-started-core-concepts-gf-access Coefficients and evaluation

  `getData()` exposes coefficients. Evaluation at a `Geometry::Point` uses the
  element basis and its mapping. The distinction matters for higher-order
  elements, vector components, and non-nodal spaces. Component expressions such
  as `field.x()` are available for compatible vector-valued fields.

  @subsection guides-getting-started-core-concepts-trial-test Trial and test functions

  Trial and test functions construct the discrete equations symbolically:

  @code{.cpp}
  TrialFunction u(vh);
  TestFunction v(vh);
  @endcode

  The trial function represents the unknown. Its associated grid function,
  `u.getSolution()`, stores the coefficients computed by the solve. The test
  function supplies the test basis used to form the equations.

  For a linear weak problem, the discrete task is to find @f$u_h\in V_h@f$ such that
  @f[
    a(u_h,v_h)=\ell(v_h)\qquad\text{for every }v_h\in V_h.
  @f]
  With basis functions @f$\phi_j@f$ and expansion
  @f$u_h=\sum_j x_j\phi_j@f$, the system is @f$A\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{b}@f$,
  where @f$A_{ij}=a(\phi_j,\phi_i)@f$ and @f$b_i=\ell(\phi_i)@f$.
  Prescribed boundary values restrict the admissible trial and test fields.

  @section guides-getting-started-core-concepts-functions Supply coefficients and data

  Function expressions describe known source terms, material coefficients,
  and boundary values. They are evaluated at geometric points during
  integration or interpolation.

  @code{.cpp}
  RealFunction source = 1;
  RealFunction boundary([](const Geometry::Point& p) { return p.x(); });
  VectorFunction force{0, -1};
  @endcode

  `Zero()` supplies scalar zero data; `Zero(dimension)` supplies vector zero
  data. Coordinate expressions in `F`, pointwise mathematical operators,
  and complex expressions such as `Re`, `Im`, and `Conjugate` are described
  in @ref guides-variational and the @ref Rodin::Variational "Variational reference".

  @section guides-getting-started-core-concepts-problem Express the weak problem

  @subsection guides-getting-started-core-concepts-operators Operators and integrals

  Expressions retain the mathematical structure until evaluation is required.
  `Grad(u)` is the gradient of a scalar field; pairing it with `Grad(v)` inside
  an integral gives the Poisson stiffness form:

  @code{.cpp}
  Problem poisson(u, v);
  poisson = Integral(Grad(u), Grad(v)) - Integral(source, v)
             + DirichletBC(u, Zero());
  @endcode

  `Integral` integrates over cells. `BoundaryIntegral` integrates over exterior
  faces; natural boundary loads enter through such boundary terms. Essential
  boundary conditions constrain the unknown. Selecting an attribute with
  `.on(attribute)` or `.over(attribute)` requires that the mesh already carries
  the intended labels. See @ref guides-boundary-conditions.

  @subsection guides-getting-started-core-concepts-problem-assembly From forms to a system

  The expression represents the residual @f$a(u_h,v_h)-\ell(v_h)@f$ together
  with constraints. Assembly evaluates local contributions using quadrature,
  combines them into the matrix and vector, and applies the constraints.
  The minus sign on the source therefore yields a positive right-hand side
  in @f$A\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{b}@f$.

  @subsection guides-getting-started-core-concepts-solving Choose a compatible solver

  The Poisson problem with these boundary conditions gives a symmetric positive
  definite system, so conjugate gradient is appropriate:

  @code{.cpp}
  Solver::CG solver(poisson);
  solver.solve();
  @endcode

  Check `solver.success()` before treating `u.getSolution()` as a completed
  result. Matrix properties determine solver choice; CG is not a general
  solver for nonsymmetric or indefinite systems. See @ref guides-solvers.

  @section guides-getting-started-core-concepts-workflow Interpret and export the solution

  The computed grid function can be evaluated, used in another form, or
  exported with its mesh:

  @code{.cpp}
  IO::XDMF xdmf("Poisson");
  xdmf.grid().setMesh(mesh).add("u", u.getSolution());
  xdmf.write().flush();
  @endcode

  The explicit name `"u"` labels the exported field. Alternatively, set a
  grid-function name before calling the unnamed `add(field)` overload.
  Meshes, spaces, and fields must remain valid while the writer observes them.
  See @ref guides-io for file formats and visualization.

  @section guides-getting-started-core-concepts-advanced Continue to specialized formulations

  Once the local conforming workflow is understood, these guides introduce
  additional numerical requirements:

  - @ref guides-interfaces-dg — Traces, jumps, averages, and facet integrals.
  - @ref guides-variational — Vector and complex expressions and integral forms.
  - @ref examples-index-integral — Integral-potential source examples.
  - @ref examples-index-surfevol — Advection and surface-evolution source examples.
  - @ref guides-backends — OpenMP, MPI, and PETSc execution paths.

  @section guides-getting-started-core-concepts-next Numerical contracts

  Read @ref guides-numerical-contracts before relying on coefficient layouts,
  projection semantics, assembly reuse, or nonlinear tangent consistency.
  These contracts connect the mathematical formulation to the behavior of the
  numerical implementation.
*/
